Notes that will save you an hour:

  * `advance`, not `next` — `next` trips clippy::should_implement_trait, and
    clippy runs with -D warnings.
  * `peek`/`advance` return `Option<&'t Token>` borrowed from the SLICE, not
    from the cursor, so you can hold the token and still call `&mut self`
    methods. That is deliberate; do not "fix" it into `&'_ Token`.
  * `expected(what)` is the one error shape: "expected {what}, found {tok:?}",
    or "... found end of input". Pass a phrase, not a word: `ident("a column
    name in the INSERT list")`. Every truncated statement is a normal Err —
    nothing indexes past the end, and there are corpus cases for it.
  * `expect(&Token::Values)` names the token by Debug ("expected Values,
    found ..."), which is fine for a keyword; use `ident(..)` when you want
    prose.
  * Cursor is Clone, cheap, so try-and-backtrack is `let save = c.clone();`.

WHAT parse_expr GIVES YOU

  Precedence, loosest first, per docs/quern.md §1:
    OR < AND < NOT < (= <> < >) < (+ -) < (* /) < unary - < primary
  Primary is a literal, `a`, `t.a`, `(expr)` or an aggregate call. Columns are
  always `Expr::Column { table, name }` — never ColumnRef, that is
  plan-logical's to write. Literals go through Value, so `'it''s'` is already
  unescaped by the lexer.

  An aggregate parses as `Expr::Agg(..)` and is REJECTED by my clause helper in
  WHERE/ON/GROUP BY/ORDER BY (quern has no HAVING). parse_expr itself does NOT
  reject one, because it cannot know the clause. If you parse an INSERT value,
  an UPDATE SET right-hand side or a DELETE predicate, check it yourself:

      let e = parse_expr(&mut c)?;
      if e.contains_agg() {
          return Err(QuernError::Parse(
              "aggregate not allowed in UPDATE SET".to_string()));
      }

  `Expr::contains_agg()` is ast's, already there. exec::eval errors on a bare
  Agg, so a missed check is a runtime Type error instead of a parse error.

