THE WHOLE API

    pub type TxnId = u64;                      // unique within ONE process run

    pub struct TxnState { /* private: Option<TxnId> + a counter from 1 */ }
    impl TxnState {
        pub fn new() -> Self;                  // also Default
        pub fn current(&self) -> Option<TxnId>;
        pub fn is_open(&self) -> bool;
        pub fn begin(&mut self) -> Result<TxnId>;      // Err(Txn) if one is open
        pub fn end(&mut self, what: &str) -> Result<TxnId>;  // Err(Txn) if none
    }

    pub trait TxnHost {
        // you implement these four accessors:
        fn txn(&mut self) -> &mut TxnState;
        fn wal(&mut self) -> &mut Wal;
        fn flush(&mut self) -> Result<()>;     // Pager::flush
        fn discard(&mut self) -> Result<()>;   // see DISCARD below — not optional

        // you get these four, and should not reimplement them:
        fn begin(&mut self) -> Result<TxnId>;
        fn commit(&mut self) -> Result<()>;
        fn rollback(&mut self) -> Result<()>;
        fn statement<T>(&mut self, body: impl FnOnce(&mut Self, TxnId) -> Result<T>) -> Result<T>;
        fn recover(&mut self, apply: impl FnMut(&mut Self, &WalRecord) -> Result<()>) -> Result<usize>;
    }

`Result` is crate::types::Result; every error is QuernError::Txn. Nothing panics.
The trait is not object-safe (two generic methods) — it is meant to be `impl`ed
on the concrete storage struct, not boxed.

HOW A Storage IMPLEMENTATION COMPOSES IT  (storage-trait: this is all of it)

    impl TxnHost for HeapStorage {
        fn txn(&mut self) -> &mut TxnState { &mut self.txn }
        fn wal(&mut self) -> &mut Wal { &mut self.wal }
        fn flush(&mut self) -> Result<()> { self.pager.flush() }
        fn discard(&mut self) -> Result<()> { /* see below */ }
    }

    impl Storage for HeapStorage {
        fn begin(&mut self)    -> Result<()> { TxnHost::begin(self).map(|_| ()) }
        fn commit(&mut self)   -> Result<()> { TxnHost::commit(self) }
        fn rollback(&mut self) -> Result<()> { TxnHost::rollback(self) }

        fn insert(&mut self, table: &str, row: &Row) -> Result<RowId> {
            self.statement(|s, txn_id| {
                s.wal().append(txn_id, KIND_INSERT, table, &payload)?;
                s.heap_insert(table, row)          // your own inherent method
            })
        }
        // update/delete the same shape. `statement` is the implicit-transaction
        // helper §4 asks for: it joins an open explicit txn, or opens one,
        // commits it on success and rolls it back on failure. Wrap each mutator
        // in it once — do not open/commit by hand at any call site.
    }

Accessors, not `&mut self` passed in, precisely so you can call
`self.statement(...)` from inside a `&mut self` method without borrowck fighting
you: each `self.txn()` / `self.wal()` borrow ends inside the call.

THE ORDERING RULE, NOW ENFORCED BY CODE

`TxnHost::commit` is:

    let id = self.txn().end("COMMIT")?;
    self.wal().commit(id)?;   // 1. REDO log durable (appends the commit record + fsync)
    self.flush()              // 2. then the data pages

wal.rs asked for exactly this order and could only ask in prose. It now lives in
one provided method, so no Storage impl writes the sequence and inverts it.
Reversed, a crash between the two leaves flushed pages that no commit record
vouches for, indistinguishable from a rolled-back transaction's.

ROLLBACK CALLS NOTHING IN THE WAL

The absence of the commit record IS the rollback (identical bytes to a kill -9,
discarded by the same `committed.contains` filter in replay). There is no undo
record and no abort marker. `rollback()` ends the txn and calls `discard()`.

DISCARD — the one way this design can silently leak uncommitted work

`Pager` buffers writes in a dirty-page map AND `read_page` serves from it, so a
rolled-back statement's pages stay visible in memory and would be written out by
the next unrelated `flush()`. `discard()` is a required method for that reason.

A blunt discard is correct: because `commit()` always flushes, nothing dirty at
rollback time belongs to a committed transaction. Cheapest honest implementation
is to reopen the `Pager` — that drops the dirty map *and* the in-memory
page_count that `allocate_page` bumped, which clearing the map alone would leave
behind. Any heap/B-tree cache above the pager must be dropped here too. A
`Pager::discard_dirty()` (clear the map, restore page_count) would be cheaper;
pager.rs has no such method today, and I did not add one to a file I do not own.

If `discard()` is left as a no-op, 100_update_delete.slt cases 20-21 (a failed
UPDATE must leave no partial work) will fail, and so will the crash test.

RECOVERY — call it once, right after open, and it must be this call

    let n = storage.recover(|s, rec| s.reapply(rec))?;   // returns records applied

It does: `wal.replay()` -> apply each record -> `flush()` -> `wal.checkpoint()`.

recovery-test: the checkpoint at the end is a correctness requirement, not
tidiness, and it is why I did NOT persist the txn_id counter. The counter here
restarts at 1 every process. With records left in the log:
  * an OLD COMMITTED txn 1 + a LIVE UNCOMMITTED txn 1 -> replay resurrects the
    live one's records; and
  * an OLD UNCOMMITTED txn 1 + a LIVE txn 1 that commits -> the old records
    become "committed" and get resurrected.
Seeding the counter from `replay()` cannot close the second case, because replay
deliberately never shows uncommitted ids. Truncating the log closes both at once,
needs no second file to keep in sync, and wal.rs wants it anyway (the log would
otherwise grow forever, and re-applying an insert allocates a FRESH RowId, so
replay is not idempotent and must not happen twice).

`src/txn.rs` test `recovery_checkpoints_so_a_reused_txn_id_cannot_resurrect_live_work`
builds both halves: it asserts the resurrection happens without recover(), and
does not with it. Worth reading before you write the kill -9 test — the window
you want is after COMMIT returns and before pager.flush() completes, and reopen
must be Wal::open -> recover(...) (replay, re-apply, flush, checkpoint).

WHAT I DID NOT BUILD
  - No savepoints, no statement-level rollback inside an explicit transaction: a
    failed statement inside BEGIN leaves the transaction OPEN and its partial
    work in it, for the client to COMMIT or ROLLBACK. A failed nested BEGIN
    leaves the outer transaction usable (090_txn.slt 7-8, 31-33) — tested.
  - No read locks, no isolation levels: single writer, §4.
  - Nothing decides whether DDL is transactional (bead .54). txn.rs is agnostic:
    if CREATE TABLE goes through `statement()` and appends a WAL record it is
    transactional, if it writes the catalog directly it is not. That choice is
    the storage/catalog layer's, not mine.

plan-mod: nothing in here needs planner changes. The one thing to know is that
BEGIN/COMMIT/ROLLBACK are not LogicalPlan variants in §3, so they cannot be
lowered — route them straight to Storage::begin/commit/rollback from wherever
statements are dispatched, and let every other statement go through the storage
layer's `statement()` wrapper, which is where implicit transactions happen.

